    "text": "Why is the SpO2 target for a known COPD patient set at 88–92% rather than the standard ≥ 94%?",
    "questionStyle": "standard",
    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "NEMSES 2021 — EMT Instructional Guidelines, Pharmacology Ch.6; Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.13 Medications",
    "explanation": "Some COPD patients with chronic hypercapnia rely on hypoxic drive — low PaO2 — as their primary stimulus to breathe. Over-oxygenating these patients can suppress this drive, reduce respiratory effort, worsen CO2 retention, and precipitate respiratory failure. Titrating oxygen to 88–92% in known COPD patients prevents this while still providing therapeutic benefit. Brady Ch.13 and NEMSES 2021 Ch.6 both specify this narrower target.",
