    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "ADA 2023 Standards of Care; Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.27; NEMSES 2021 Ch.6",
    "explanation": "Symptoms of hypoglycaemia typically begin to resolve as blood glucose rises above 70 mg/dL (the clinical hypoglycaemia threshold per ADA 2023). However, full cognitive recovery may lag glucose normalisation by 15–30 minutes due to residual CNS effects. Administer oral glucose gel (15–24 g carbohydrate), reassess BGL after 10–15 minutes, and reassess mental status. If patient remains symptomatic after one dose, a second dose may be given. Transport all hypoglycaemic patients regardless of apparent recovery.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "oral-glucose",
      "hypoglycaemia",
      "BGL-threshold",
      "pharmacology"
    ],
    "nremtDomain": "PATIENT_TREATMENT_TRANSPORT",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "pharmacology-emt-029-0",
        "text": "Above 70 mg/dL — this is the ADA hypoglycaemia threshold at which symptoms typically begin to resolve",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "order": 0,
        "explanation": "Correct. ADA 2023 defines hypoglycaemia as BGL <70 mg/dL. As glucose rises above this threshold, adrenergic and neuroglycopenic symptoms begin to resolve, though cognitive recovery may take 15–30 min longer."
      },
      {
        "id": "pharmacology-emt-029-1",
        "text": "Above 200 mg/dL — only full glucose normalisation reverses symptoms",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 1,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Waiting for BGL >200 is unnecessary and would delay repeat assessment. Symptoms begin resolving when glucose rises above 70 mg/dL — the established clinical threshold."
      },
      {
        "id": "pharmacology-emt-029-2",
        "text": "BGL is not reassessed — one dose of oral glucose is always sufficient",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 2,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. BGL should be reassessed 10–15 minutes after glucose administration. If symptoms persist or BGL remains <70, a second dose may be indicated per local protocol."
      },
      {
        "id": "pharmacology-emt-029-3",
        "text": "Above 40 mg/dL — this is the threshold for administering oral glucose",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 3,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. The ADA-defined hypoglycaemia threshold and the treatment trigger is <70 mg/dL. A BGL of 40 mg/dL is severely low — symptoms resolve when glucose rises above 70, not 40."
      }
    ]
