    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "ADA 2023 Standards of Care; Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.27; NEMSES 2021 Ch.6",
    "explanation": "Glucagon IM (1 mg adult, 0.5 mg child <20 kg): used when patient is too altered to safely receive oral glucose and IV access is unavailable. Mechanism: stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis (breakdown of stored glycogen → glucose release). Expected response: blood glucose rises in 10–15 minutes; patient begins to improve. Critical limitations: (1) Requires adequate hepatic glycogen stores — malnourished, alcoholic, or prolonged starvation patients may not respond; (2) Causes nausea/vomiting — position in recovery position. If no response in 15 min, a second dose is unlikely to work.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "glucagon",
      "hypoglycaemia",
      "mechanism",
      "prehospital"
    ],
    "nremtDomain": "PATIENT_TREATMENT_TRANSPORT",
