    "text": "Which paediatric airway characteristic makes foreign body airway obstruction (FBAO) more likely compared to adults?",
    "questionStyle": "standard",
    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed, Ch. 43 — Paediatric Emergencies; ACEP",
    "explanation": "Paediatric airway differences that increase FBAO risk: relatively larger tongue (occludes airway more easily), narrower subglottic space (cricoid ring is the narrowest point in children vs adults), floppy/omega-shaped epiglottis (harder to visualise), and proportionally larger head causing neck flexion in supine. The narrow cricoid ring (funnel-shaped paediatric airway vs cylindrical adult) is most relevant to obstruction.",
    "difficulty": 3,
