    "text": "During cardiac arrest, ETCO₂ readings are consistently below 10 mmHg despite CPR for 20 minutes. What does this likely indicate?",
    "questionStyle": "standard",
    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "AHA 2020 ACLS Guidelines; Falk et al., Ann Emerg Med 1988; Brady 14th Ed Ch. 26",
    "explanation": "Low persistent ETCO₂ (<10 mmHg) during CPR correlates with poor cardiac output from compressions and reduced metabolic CO₂ production. Sustained ETCO₂ <10 mmHg for >20 minutes is associated with poor outcome (sensitivity ~70–90% for nonsurvival in arrest). In-field ROSC is associated with a sudden rise in ETCO₂ to >35 mmHg. This information guides team decision-making but should not be the sole factor in terminating resuscitation.",
    "difficulty": 4,
