    "id": "respiratory-emergencies-026",
    "moduleSlug": "respiratory-emergencies",
    "text": "A patient has SpO2 of 86% despite oxygen therapy but has a normal PCO₂. What type of respiratory failure is this?",
    "questionStyle": "standard",
    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed, Ch. 16; West's Respiratory Physiology 10th Ed",
    "explanation": "Type I (hypoxaemic) respiratory failure: PaO₂ <60 mmHg (SpO₂ <90%) with normal or low PCO₂. Caused by V/Q mismatch (pneumonia, pulmonary oedema) or diffusion defect. Responds to supplemental oxygen. Type II (hypercapnic/ventilatory) failure: PaO₂ low AND PCO₂ high (>45 mmHg) — the patient cannot effectively eliminate CO₂ (COPD exacerbation, neuromuscular disease, severe asthma).",
    "difficulty": 4,
    "tags": [
      "respiratory-failure",
      "type-I",
      "hypoxaemic",
      "Type-II",
      "hypercapnic",
      "respiratory"
    ],
