    "sourceRef": "Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed, Ch. 8 — Airway Assessment; respiratory physiology",
    "explanation": "Stridor is a high-pitched, crowing/musical sound produced by turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway (larynx/subglottis). It is heard primarily during inspiration (inspiratory stridor) because negative intrathoracic pressure during inspiration tends to collapse the extrathoracic airway if obstructed. Causes: croup, epiglottitis, angioedema, foreign body at the larynx. Distinguish from wheeze (bilateral, lower airways, typically expiratory).",
