    "caseOutcome": "TIA patients transported for immediate hospital evaluation within 24 hours have a dramatically lower subsequent stroke rate. ABCD2 scoring and early MRI changed management; approximately 10-15% of TIA patients have a stroke within 90 days if untreated, but early intervention reduces this to <3% (Johnston et al., NEJM 2000; EXPRESS study 2007).",
    "sourceRef": "Johnston et al. (2000) — Short-term prognosis after emergency dept diagnosis of TIA, JAMA; NEMSES 2021 Ch.10",
    "explanation": "This presentation is a classic TIA (transient ischaemic attack): neurological deficits that resolve fully within 24 hours (usually <1 hour). The prehospital protocol for TIA is identical to stroke — document the time symptoms started AND the time they resolved, transport to stroke centre. DO NOT reassure the patient that 'it passed.' Johnston et al. 2000 showed 10% stroke risk within 3 months without intervention.",
