    "id": "anaphylaxis-034",
    "moduleSlug": "anaphylaxis",
    "text": "What is the correct injection site and angle for epinephrine auto-injector administration in anaphylaxis?",
    "questionStyle": "standard",
    "caseContext": "",
    "caseOutcome": "",
    "sourceRef": "Simons FER et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2001; WAO 2011; EpiPen prescribing information; Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.25",
    "explanation": "Epinephrine auto-injector (EpiPen): site — anterolateral mid-thigh (vastus lateralis muscle), which can be given through clothing. Angle — 90° (perpendicular to the thigh). Why anterolateral thigh: (1) Large muscle mass for reliable IM absorption; (2) Fastest systemic absorption compared to deltoid; (3) Accessible through clothing — critical in emergencies. Simons 2001 showed anterolateral thigh absorption is ~2× faster than deltoid in anaphylaxis. Do NOT inject into the buttock (risk of sciatic nerve) or into a vein. Hold in place for 3 seconds.",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "tags": [
      "epinephrine",
      "injection-site",
      "anterolateral-thigh",
      "auto-injector"
    ],
    "nremtDomain": "PATIENT_TREATMENT_TRANSPORT",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "anaphylaxis-034-0",
        "text": "Anterolateral mid-thigh, 90° angle, held 3 seconds — fastest systemic absorption; can be given through clothing",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "order": 0,
        "explanation": "Correct. Anterolateral thigh is the standard site — fastest absorption, through clothing, 90° perpendicular injection, hold 3 seconds."
      },
      {
        "id": "anaphylaxis-034-1",
        "text": "Deltoid muscle, 45° angle — same as vaccine administration technique",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 1,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. While deltoid injection is used for many IM medications, the anterolateral thigh provides faster epinephrine absorption in anaphylaxis. EpiPen instruction specifies the anterolateral thigh."
      },
      {
        "id": "anaphylaxis-034-2",
        "text": "Gluteal muscle — largest muscle provides best drug distribution",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 2,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. Gluteal injection risks sciatic nerve injury and provides slower absorption than anterolateral thigh. The gluteal site is not recommended for epinephrine auto-injectors."
      },
      {
        "id": "anaphylaxis-034-3",
        "text": "Dorsum of the forearm — away from the systemic circulation to allow controlled absorption",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 3,
        "explanation": "Incorrect. The forearm is not an IM injection site. Controlled, slow absorption is the opposite of what is needed in anaphylaxis. Rapid systemic delivery via the anterolateral thigh is essential."
      }
    ]
