    "explanation": "Biphasic anaphylaxis: a second episode of anaphylaxis occurring 1–72 hours after initial resolution, without re-exposure to the allergen. Incidence: ~5% (Simons 2010 JACI, range 1–20% in studies). Risk factors for biphasic: severe initial reaction, delayed initial epinephrine, idiopathic trigger, large allergen dose. This patient's symptoms are a textbook biphasic reaction — they must be treated identically: epinephrine IM immediately, transport to hospital. This case illustrates why all anaphylaxis patients should be observed 4–8 hours in hospital — premature discharge contributed to this outcome.",
    "difficulty": 3,
    "tags": [
      "biphasic-anaphylaxis",
      "delayed-reaction",
      "8-hours",
      "re-treatment"
    ],
    "nremtDomain": "PATIENT_TREATMENT_TRANSPORT",
