    "text": "A conscious patient who ingested a corrosive alkali (oven cleaner) one hour ago is demanding treatment. He has burns to his mouth and is drooling. Which intervention is MOST appropriate?",
    "questionStyle": "case-based",
    "caseContext": "41-year-old male ingested approximately 30 mL of commercial oven cleaner (sodium hydroxide) one hour ago. He is alert and agitated. Burns visible on lips and tongue. Excessive drooling. Denies abdominal pain. Vital signs stable. Calling for 'the charcoal' he saw on TV.",
    "caseOutcome": "Caustic alkali ingestion is treated with airway monitoring, IV access, and rapid transport per Brady Ch.30 and NEMSES 2021. Activated charcoal and emesis induction are both contraindicated. Early endoscopy in hospital determines mucosal injury extent.",
    "sourceRef": "Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.30 Poisoning and Overdose; NEMSES 2021 — EMT Instructional Guidelines, Poisoning and Overdose Ch.11",
    "explanation": "Caustic/corrosive ingestion has two critical management points: (1) Never induce emesis — re-exposure of oropharynx and airway to caustic material on the way back up causes additional burns and airway compromise; (2) Never administer activated charcoal — it does not adsorb caustics and can obscure endoscopy and cause vomiting. Monitor airway closely (oropharyngeal burns can swell rapidly), provide IV access, high-flow O2, and transport urgently.",
    "difficulty": 3,
