    "explanation": "Calcium channel blocker overdose causes bradycardia (SA/AV node suppression) and vasodilation (hypotension) by blocking L-type calcium channels in cardiac and vascular smooth muscle. Prehospital management is supportive: high-flow O2, IV access, cardiac monitoring, IV fluid bolus for hypotension, and rapid transport. Atropine may be tried for bradycardia but is often ineffective. Specific antidotes (calcium chloride/gluconate, glucagon, high-dose insulin, lipid emulsion) are hospital-based. Brady Ch.30 emphasises rapid transport.",
