    "id": "toxicology-poisoning-047",
    "moduleSlug": "toxicology-poisoning",
    "text": "An EMT at a chemical plant responds to a worker with seizure activity, excessive secretions, miosis, and bradycardia after a valve failure released liquid pesticide. Scene safety has been established. What is the correct order of prehospital intervention?",
    "questionStyle": "case-based",
    "caseContext": "38-year-old male chemical plant worker found seizing and then postictal on the floor near a ruptured organophosphate storage valve. Clothes saturated. HR 42, pupils 1 mm, profuse secretions, incontinent. HazMat team has cleared the scene. One rescuer available.",
    "caseOutcome": "Immediate clothing removal, skin decontamination, airway management, and rapid transport with ALS intercept are the documented sequence for severe organophosphate poisoning per NEMSES 2021 and Brady Ch.30. Atropine (hospital/ALS) is the antidote for muscarinic effects.",
