    "text": "Using START triage, categorise the following 8 patients from this scenario. Which patient is assigned RED (immediate)?",
    "questionStyle": "case-based",
    "caseContext": "A two-vehicle high-speed collision with 8 occupants. Patient A: walking, holding wrist. Patient B: supine, RR 32, radial pulse present, follows commands. Patient C: supine, apnoeic after airway repositioning. Patient D: RR 22, no radial pulse, does not follow commands. Patient E: RR 18, radial pulse present, follows commands — leg fracture only. Patient F: supine, RR 22, radial pulse present, follows commands — facial lacerations. Patient G: RR 15, radial pulse present, does not follow commands. Patient H: supine, RR 28, radial pulse present, follows commands.",
    "caseOutcome": "First arriving EMS unit completed START triage in under 4 minutes, tagged Patients B, D, and G as RED, Patient C as BLACK, Patients E, F, H as YELLOW, and Patient A as GREEN. ALS and additional BLS resources were requested. Patients B, D, G transported to trauma centre by air and ground ALS.",
    "sourceRef": "NEMSES 2021 — EMT Instructional Guidelines, MCI Ch.15; Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.48",
    "explanation": "Patient B: RR 32 (>30) = RED immediately. Patient C: apnoeic after repositioning = BLACK. Patient D: RR 22 (normal), no radial pulse = RED. Patient G: RR 15 (normal), radial pulse present, unable to follow commands = RED. Patient A: ambulatory = GREEN. Patients E, F, H: all RPM criteria within normal limits = YELLOW. START triage is completed systematically with one criterion immediately triggering the category.",
