    "id": "ems-operations-032",
    "moduleSlug": "ems-operations",
    "text": "Based on this hazmat scenario, what is the correct immediate action for the arriving EMS unit?",
    "questionStyle": "case-based",
    "caseContext": "You are dispatched to a chemical manufacturing plant where workers report a 'gas leak.' On approach 0.5 km away you observe a cloud of yellowish-green vapour drifting from the facility. Multiple workers are running from the building. Wind is from the south-southwest. The facility is located on low ground. Several workers are collapsed near the plant entrance. Dispatch cannot confirm the chemical involved.",
    "caseOutcome": "The first EMS unit staged upwind, uphill, and upstream (north of the facility) at 800 metres. They contacted CHEMTREC (1-800-424-9300) and requested HazMat and Fire response. They did not enter the Hot Zone. Decon teams extracted workers. EMS treated patients in the Cold Zone after decontamination.",
    "sourceRef": "Brady Emergency Care 14th Ed — Ch.51 HazMat; DOT Emergency Response Guidebook 2024 Edition; NEMSES 2021 — EMT Instructional Guidelines, EMS Operations Ch.3",
    "explanation": "EMS must stage upwind, uphill, and upstream of the incident before any action. Do not approach the collapsed workers without HazMat PPE — they are in the Hot Zone. Contact CHEMTREC for identification and guidance. Request HazMat and Fire resources. The yellowish-green cloud suggests chlorine or similar halogen gas. Patients cannot be treated until decontaminated. EMTs do not enter the Hot or Warm zones.",
    "difficulty": 4,
    "tags": [
      "HazMat",
      "case-based",
      "scene-positioning",
      "cold-zone",
      "CHEMTREC"
    ],
    "nremtDomain": "OPERATIONS",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "ems-operations-032-0",
        "text": "Stage upwind, uphill, and upstream; do not approach; contact CHEMTREC; request HazMat resources",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "order": 1,
        "explanation": "EMS must establish safe positioning, not approach contaminated victims, and activate appropriate specialist resources."
      },
      {
        "id": "ems-operations-032-1",
        "text": "Drive to the plant entrance and begin treating the collapsed workers immediately",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 2,
        "explanation": "Approaching without PPE in a contaminated environment would expose the crew to the hazardous agent — rescuers would become secondary victims."
      },
      {
        "id": "ems-operations-032-2",
        "text": "Don N95 masks and enter the building to rescue trapped workers",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 3,
        "explanation": "N95 masks provide no protection against toxic gases; only Level A or B HazMat PPE provides adequate protection in a chemical Hot Zone."
      },
      {
        "id": "ems-operations-032-3",
        "text": "Stage directly downwind to observe the vapour cloud direction for scene safety",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "order": 4,
        "explanation": "Staging downwind places the crew directly in the toxic vapour path — EMS always stages upwind."
      }
    ]
